  "case": {
    "presentation": "Patient is a 55 year old woman with a history of fecal transplant for Clostridium difficile infection. The patient developed an atypical pneumonia with diarrhea and was started on azithromycin after which she had loose and bloody stools. The bloody stools have resolved but she has persistent loose stool. She has no watery stool, abdominal pain, fevers, chills, or other GI symptoms. Do we need to order C. Diff testing for this patient?",
    "recommendations": "Given the patient's history of C. difficile infection and recent exposure to antibiotics, a recurrence is a consideration. However, the current symptoms do not meet the typical criteria for testing (e.g., high volume watery stools >3 times per day, significant abdominal pain, bloating). It is reasonable to monitor the patient in routine follow-up and consider testing only if symptoms worsen or meet institutional testing criteria. Antibiotic-associated diarrhea without C. difficile is also a likely possibility. If bleeding recurs, endoscopy referral is warranted. "
